'''Sanyan''' ({{Audio|kz-Sanyan.ogg|pronunciation}}; {{Langx|kz|ߌߛ߭ߐߞߊ߲ ߊߜߓߊߙߊ|Sànyán}}), officially the '''State of Sanyan''' ({{Langx|kz|ߌߖߐߞߊߣߌ ߛߊߣߦߊ߲|Ìjẹ́ Kànì Sànyán}}) is a {{W|sovereignty|sovereign}} {{W|island country|island city-state}}, consisting of an archipelago located roughly {{convert|20|km}} west of [[Ayorin]] on the [[Kaftia|Kaftian mainland]]. The sole official language is [[Kinza language|Kinza]], while around 30% of the current population is at least conversational in a second language, including [[W:Yoruba language|Imo]], [[W:Igbo language|Ekwe]] and [[Maroniko language|Maroniko]]. Sanyan's ancient [[Kinza script]] (Sanyanian script) is one of the oldest alphabets still in use ([[W:Sacred language|liturgically]]). The {{W|Yoruba calendar|Kinza Calender}} (''Kọ́jọ́dá''), which is approximately seven years and three months behind the [[w:Gregorian calendar|Gregorian calendar]], is given state preference. A majority of the population adheres to the traditional {{W|Yoruba religion|Òrìṣà-Ifá}} whereas a significant minority are followers of another religion, to include {{W|Islam}}, {{W|Christianity}} and {{W|Judaism}} (primarily of [[Imo Jews|Imo descent]]).

The archipelago was unified in 1137 following the conquests of [[Tiwalade of Sanyan|Tiwalade]]. Throughout the 16th and 17th centuries, Sanyan faced pressure from the Breisland and other Illypnian colonial powers that desired [[W:Slave labor|slave labor]]; nevertheless, it successfully resisted colonization and preserved it's sovereignty during the [[Conquest of North Kaftia]]. Petroleum was discovered in 1896 and followed up by [[Petroleum industry in Ayorin|several other finds]] in the [[Bight of Ayorin]]. Oil drilling began in 1908 under the guidance of the [[Kafto-Araucarian Company|Kafto-Araucarian Petroleum Company]], and expanded throughout the next decade, avoiding [[New Imperialism|Illypnian imperialism]] conquest due to [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter|amicable protection from Balisca]]. It would later [[W:Nationalization|nationalize]] the company in 1921 following the [[Baliscan Revolution]]. Sanyan has since become one of the world's largest oil producers and the largest oil exporters, controlling the world's <>-largest oil reserves and the <>-largest gas reserves. Traditionally fueled by these natural resources, Sanyan's economy is currently expanding in the sectors of science, tourism, commerce and medical tourism. The state is categorized as a World Bank high income economy. The state government has attracted criticism for a variety of its policies, including its longstanding support for [[Pan-Kaftianism]], alleged sponsorship of international [[Black nationalism|Black nationalist]] organizations, and its adherence to the ''[[Obodo àlàáfíà]]'' ideology.

In the 21st century, Sanyan is considered a {{W|middle power}} in Kaftia through its resource wealth and continued expansion of its state-backed media group [[Noya]] across the [[Kaftian diaspora]]. The Sanyanian economy is the ?-largest in Central Kaftia, and the world's <>-largest economy by PPP. As a country with a very high {{W|Human Development Index}}, it offers a tuition-free university education, no {{W|personal income tax}}, and a free {{W|universal health care system}}. Proportionally, Sanyan is home to the world's third-largest immigrant population. It also has one of the world's youngest populations, with approximately 50 per cent of its population of being under 25 years old. In addition to being a member of the [[Alaafia Council]], Sanyan is an active and founding member of the [[Kaftian Union]], [[Concepción Organization of Cooperation|COCO]] and has observer status in <>, <> and <>.

==Etymology==
The name ''{{Linktext|sanyan}}'' is widely accepted by linguists and historians to be derived from the Imo word ''sányán'', referring to a specific type of raw, wild silk harvested from the cocoons of the [[W:Anaphe (moth)|''Anaphe'' moth]]. Prior to the unification of the archipelago in 1137, the central island served as a regional hub for the textile industry in the [[W:Bight of Benin|Bight of Benin]]. Sanyanian weavers were renowned along the West Kaftian coast for their production of this durable, beige-colored cloth, which was highly valued by nobility on the mainland. 

Early Volisanian records from 1472 refer to the archipelago as ''Illa de Seda'' ("Isles of Silk") before adopting the nativized transliteration ''Sanyá'' or ''Sanyian'' in later navigational charts. The demonym for a citizen of the state is Sanyanian, while the adjective relating to the country is Sanyan. In the [[Kinza language]], citizens of pre-colonial descent are known as [[Olùkínza people|''Olùkínza'']] ({{Literal translation|keepers of [[Kinza]]}}), while all citizens of Sanyan (inclusive of [[Ethnography of Sanyan|all backgrounds]]) are referred to as ''Ọmọ-Sanyan'' ("children of Sanyan").

==History==
{{Main|History of Sanyan}}The [[Sanyanian archipelago]] consists of approximately 60 islands formed through volcanic activity and subsequent erosion over millions of years. The two main islands—[[Olayema]] and [[Àlà-tuntun]]—account for roughly 85% of the archipelago's total land area of {{convert|165.03|km2|sqmi}}. Numerous smaller islets and barrier islands surround the main islands, creating a complex network of sheltered waterways and natural harbors that would prove crucial to Sanyan's development as a maritime power.

Prior to urbanization, the archipelago was heavily forested with dense [[W:Tropical rainforest|tropical rainforest]] covering the interior of the main islands. The forests provided abundant resources for early inhabitants, including timber for construction and boat-building, wild game, medicinal plants, and the crucial [[W:Anaphe (moth)|Anaphe moth]] cocoons that would form the basis of Sanyan's famous [[Sanyanian silk|silk industry]]. Fresh water streams descended from the volcanic highlands, providing reliable water sources and supporting fertile agricultural lands in the coastal lowlands. The surrounding waters were rich with fish and shellfish, while the barrier islands and coral reefs created protected lagoons that were idea for fishing and other maritime activities. The natural harbor formed by the configuration of the islands would later become one of the most important ports on the Kaftian coast.

=== The foundation myth of Olokun ===
{{Main|Olókun|Òrìṣà-Ifá}}

[[File:Vision of Olokun.png|left|thumb|298x298px|The [[Vision of Olokun|'''''Ìríràn-Olókun''''']], the legendary event that led to the settlement of the Sanyanian archipelago]]
According to [[Sanyanian oral tradition]] as preserved by the [[W:Griot|arokin]], the archipelago was first settled under the guidance of [[W:Olokun|Olókun]], the [[W:Orisa|Orisa]] of the sea, its depths, and wealth. In Sanyanian tradition, Olókun is depicted as a [[W:Gender fluid|fluid deity]], neither strictly male nor female but embodying both aspects and all points between—a reflection of the nature of the sea itself. The foundation myth references a time when the ancestors of the Sanyanians lived on the mainland of what is now [[Ayorin]], facing increasing conflict, drought, and the encroachment of hostile military powers from the south. According to the tradition, a [[W:Babalawo|''babalawo'']] or [[W:Iyalawo|''iyalawo'']] of Olókun shrine received a vision in which the deity appeared rising from the waters, instructing them to guide the people to the "isles of shelter and abundance across the sea".

The legend recounts that Olókun led the people across the waters, appearing as a shimmering presence upon the waves by day and as a glowing light upon the horizon by night. After several days of sailing in dugout canoes, the people sighted the forested peaks of the islands rising from the sea. Olókun was said to have calmed the waters around the islands, creating the sheltered harbor that would become the heart of Sanyanian civilization. Upon landing, the people are said to have found the islands uninhabited, rich with [[W:Fresh water|fresh water]], game, and the silk-producing [[W:Anaphe (moth)|''Anaphe'' moths]] whose cocoons would become central to Sanyanian future prosperity. In gratitude, the settlers established the first shrine to Olókun on [[Ìwọ̀rìkò Island]], beginning a tradition of worship and sacrifice that continues to the present day.

==== Historical interpretation ====
While the foundation myth contains legendary elements, historians and archaeologists generally accept that the archipelago was indeed uninhabited prior to the arrival of [[Proto-Ayoritic peoples]] around the 1st millennium BCE. The myth likely preserves [[W:Collective memory|collective memory]] of an actual migration from the mainland, possibly driven by environmental pressures, political conflicts, or the search for new resources. The emphasis on Olókun's guidance reflects the maritime orientation that would characterize Sanyanian culture throughout its history.

=== Early settlement period (c. 1000 BCE – 800 CE) ===
Archaeological evidence indicates that the first permanent settlements on the archipelago were established by [[Proto-Ayoritic peoples]] sometime around the [[1st millennium BCE]]. These early settlers likely arrived from the mainland of what is now Ayorin, following the pattern of [[W:Fish migration|migrating fish]] other seafood resources islands' abundant resources and defensible position. The earliest settlements were concentrated along the protected eastern coasts of [[Olayema]], where the natural harbor provided safe anchorage for fishing boats and the surrounding waters offered rich fishing grounds. Early inhabitants lived in small communities of extended families, practicing a combination of fishing, hunting, and gathering. 

The Sanyanian diet was heavily based on indigenous staples like [[W:Yam (vegetable)|yams]], [[W:Millet|millet]], [[W:Sorghum|sorghum]], [[Kaftian rice]], [[W:Plantain|plantains]], and [[W:Black-eyed pea|black-eyed peas]]. Before the introduction of [[W:Cassava|cassava]] and [[W:Maize|maize]] (which came later through trade), diets relied on these native crops along with [[W:Palm oil|palm oil]], diverse vegetables, and indigenous spices such as [[W:Alligator pepper|alligator pepper]], [[W:Locust bean|locust beans]] ([[W:Daddawa|daddawa]]), and [[W:Calabash nutmeg|calabash nutmeg]]. [[W:Fish|Fish]] and other seafood were central to the diet, reflective of the archipelago's maritime orientation. Various preparation techniques were developed, including smoking, drying, and fermenting fish for preservation. Meat from hunting and livestock was consumed less frequently but often formed part of ceremonial feasts. Cooking techniques emphasized [[W:One-pot dishes|one-pot dishes]] that could be prepared over open fires, with [[W:Stews|stews]] and [[W:Porridges|porridges]] forming the basis of most meals. This cuisine reflected both the islands' limited resources and the cultural influences carried by the early settlers from mainland Kaftia.
{{Main|Kinza script}}

One of the most significant developments of the early settlement period was the creation of the [[Kinza script]] around the [[1st century BCE]]. The script is one of the oldest alphabets still in use, though today it is primarily employed for [[W:Liturgy|liturgical]] purposes. The origins of the script remain somewhat obscure, but it appears to have developed independently, possibly inspired by earlier [[W:Proto-writing|proto-writing]] systems used for recording trade transactions. The Kinza script was initially used for commercial purposes—recording inventories, trade agreements, and genealogies—but gradually expanded to encompass religious, historical, and literary texts. The development of writing contributed significantly to the archipelago's growing sophistication and its ability to maintain records across generations outside of the reliance on the arokin.

==== Emergence of the silk industry ====
{{Main|Sanyanian silk}}

[[File:Reticulate Bagnet (Anaphe reticulata) (16547029717).jpg|thumb|The [[W:Anaphe (moth)|Anaphe moth]], from which the early settlers of Sanyan retrieved silk for their [[Sanyanian silk|renowned garments]] ]]
The silk industry that would become central to Sanyan's early mercantile development and identity would emerge during this early period. The [[Anaphe (moth)|Anaphe moth]], native to the archipelago's tropical forests, produces cocoons of raw silk that can be harvested and processed into a distinctive, durable fabric. Unlike the cultivated silk of [[Central Fosia|Central]] and [[East Fosia]], Sanyanian silk is wild-harvested, giving it unique properties and a characteristic beige color.

Early inhabitants discovered the properties of Anaphe silk and developed techniques for harvesting the cocoons, processing the silk fibers, and weaving them into cloth. By the early centuries CE, Sanyanian silk cloth was being produced in sufficient quantities to trade with mainland communities. The fabric's durability and distinctive appearance made it highly valued, and the silk trade would become the foundation of Sanyan's prosperity.

==== Religious development ====
{{Main|Òrìṣà-Ifá}}

The religious traditions that would coalesce into [[Òrìṣà-Ifá]] emerged during this period. While the fundamental elements of the religion originated on the mainland of Ayorin around [[500 BCE]] as part of the [[Ọ̀mìlì traditional religion]], the maritime separation of the Sanyanian communities led to the development of distinctive local traditions. [[Olókun]], as the [[W:Patron deity|patron deity]] of the Sanyanian people, received particular veneration, but the full pantheon of [[Orisa]] were recognized and worshipped. The role of the arokin (griots) in preserving religious knowledge, history, and cultural values became firmly established during this period, setting patterns that would continue throughout Sanyanian history.

=== City-state period (800 – 1126 CE) ===

==== Emergence of urban centers ====
By the [[8th century]], the small fishing and farming communities of the early settlement period had coalesced into larger urban centers. These [[City-state|city-states]], typically centered on a natural harbor or defensible position, were characterized by sophisticated political organization, specialized economies, and distinctive local identities. The city-states were mercantile and trade-oriented from their inception, establishing mutually-benefitial relationships with not only each other, but with various coastal societies on the [[Kaftia|Kaftian mainland]]. Unlike the expansive territorial kingdoms of the mainland, Sanyanian polities were [[W:Thalassocracy|thalassocratic]]—exercising power through utilization of trade networks rather than through control of expansive land areas. Each city-state maintained its own fleet of trading canoes, its own commercial relationships with mainland partners, and its own system of governance.

==== Major city-states ====

===== Tòkunbọ́ =====
{{Main|Tòkunbọ́}}

The most powerful of the pre-unification city-states, [[Tòkunbọ́]] was situated on the eastern coast of Olayema, commanding the archipelago's finest natural harbor. The city's name, meaning "the returner of wealth," reflected its role as the primary [[W:Entrepôt|entrepôt]] for trade between the islands and the mainland. Tòkunbọ́ controlled much of the silk trade and maintained extensive commercial relationships with the [[Owuro Empire]] and other mainland powers. The city was governed by an [[Ọba-Òkun]] ({{Literal translation|sacred chief of the sea}}), advised by a council of lineage heads ([[Olórí-Ọ̀bọ̀]]) and representatives of the merchant guilds. Tales of Tòkunbọ́'s prosperity attracted migrants from across the archipelago and even reached coastal communities on the mainland, making it the largest and most cosmopolitan of the city-states.

===== Ìlú-Ayo =====
{{Main|Ìlú-Ayo}}

Located on the western coast of Olayema, [[Ìlú-Ayo]] ({{lang|kz|"city of joy"}}) was known for its textile production and its skilled weavers. While less commercially powerful than Tòkunbọ́, Ìlú-Ayo developed a reputation for producing the finest silk cloth in the archipelago. The city was also a center of religious learning, with important temples and a tradition of priestly scholarship.

===== Ojore =====
{{Main|Ojore|University of Ojore}}

Situated on the northern coast of Àlà-tuntun, [[Ojore]] was distinguished by its scholarly traditions. The city would later become the site of the [[University of Ojore]], founded in [[1129]]. Ojore's rulers emphasized learning and religious study, and the city attracted scholars, priests, and arokin from across the archipelago and beyond.

===== Ẹ̀yọ́-Ìbà =====
{{Main|Ẹ̀yọ́-Ìbà}}
[[File:Ìwọ̀rìkò Island.png|thumb|The shrine complex dedicated to [[Olókun]] on [[Ìwọ̀rìkò Island]], under the influence of [[Ẹ̀yọ́-Ìbà]]]]
A smaller city-state on the southern coast of Olayema, [[Ẹ̀yọ́-Ìbà]] ({{Literal translation|hill of reverence}}) was known for its religious significance. The city was the location of a handful of important shrines and temples, including the primary sanctuary of Olókun on nearby Ìwọ̀rìkò Island. The priests of Ẹ̀yọ́-Ìbà exercised considerable influence across the archipelago through their control of religious institutions.

Pre-unification Sanyanian city-states shared broadly similar political structures, though with local variations. At the apex of each polity stood the Ọba-Òkun, a sacred chief whose authority derived from both political and religious sources. The Ọba-Òkun was typically selected from a royal lineage by a council of kingmakers ([[Àjẹ́-Amọ̀ye]]), ensuring that the position remained within established families while preventing simple hereditary succession.

Power was not absolute. The Ọba-Òkun was balanced by multiple councils and institutions:

* '''[[Olórí-Ọ̀bọ̀]]''' — Lineage heads who represented the major families and clans
* '''[[Alájèpà]]''' — Trade guilds that controlled commercial activities and represented merchant interests
* '''[[Àjẹ́-Àṣírí]]''' — Secret societies that played important roles in governance, justice, and social control
* '''[[Ìwàrẹ́ fà]]''' — Judicial and religious councils that interpreted law and tradition

This system of distributed authority reflected influences from both [[W:Yoruba people|Imo]] and [[W:Igbo people|Ekwe]] political traditions, adapted to the particular circumstances of island life. The result was a political culture that valued consensus, consultation, and the balancing of competing interests.

==== Economic and social development ====
The city-state period saw dramatic expansion of Sanyan's economy. The emerging architecture of the city-states reflected both practical needs and cultural values; typical houses consisted of rectangular windowless single-room buildings arranged around a central courtyard ringed by [[W:Verandas|verandas]], and building styles resembled those of the Owuro, including construction from earth, wood, [[W:Palm oil|palm oil]], and straw bolstered by timber frameworks and roofed with thatched leaves and wood, or later aluminum and corrugated iron. Most medieval and pre-colonial Sanyanian settlements would be surrounded by defensive mud walls, while more developed settlements (such as Tòkunbọ́) typically erected a series of fortifications equipped with guard houses and [[W:Moat|moats]]. The walls served both defensive purposes and as markers of community or political boundaries.

Public buildings included temples, council houses, and the prominent compounds of the Ọba-Òkun. These structures were typically larger and more elaborately decorated than private dwellings of commoners, sporting carved wooden pillars, painted walls, and decorative elements reflecting religious and cultural themes, such as extensive use of the [[Kinza script]] and statues dedicated to the [[W:Orisa|Orisa]].

The silk trade would continue to grow in scale and sophistication as the city-states continued to centralize, with Sanyanian silk and cloth reaching markets far beyond its immediate region. By the [[12th century]], Sanyanian silk had acquired a reputation that extended to the [[Mesogean Sea]], carried by traders of the [[Empire of Burawa|Burawi Empire]] who served as intermediaries between Sanyan and more distant markets.

Other industries developed alongside silk production. [[W:Copper|Copper]] and [[W:Iron|iron]] working were practiced, using metals imported from the mainland. [[W:Pottery|Pottery]], [[W:Basket weaving|basketry]], and [[W:Woodworking|woodworking]] all reached sophisticated levels of craftsmanship. The fishing industry expanded to meet the needs of growing urban populations. Trade with the mainland continued to be conducted primarily through the Owuro Empire, which maintained its dominance of the the coastal regions. Sanyanian traders exchanged their silk cloth, [[W:Cowrie shell|cowrie shells]] (which served as currency throughout much of Kaftia), and other products such as fish for mainland goods including metals, [[W:Salt|salt]], and agricultural products. The relationship was mutually beneficial but created dependencies that would prove significant as mainland politics evolved.

=== Relations with the Kaftian mainland ===
{{Main|Owuro Empire|Imfezzan raids on Sanyan}}

The [[Owuro Empire]] was the dominant mainland power during much of the city-state period, and Sanyan's relationship with Owuro was central to its political and economic life. The relationship was primarily commercial—Owuro served as Sanyan's gateway to mainland markets and as the source of many essential imports, such as [[tba]], [[tba]], and [[tba]]. However, the relationship was not without tension. The Owuro Empire was engaged in [[Owuro conflicts in Central Kaftia|continuous conflicts]] with neighboring powers, including the expanding [[Empire of Burawa|Burawi Empire]] to the south, [[Imfezzan people|Imfezzan]] raiders from the north, and rebelling Ekwe tribes in the southeast. These conflicts would often disrupte trade routes and created refugees who sought shelter in Sanyan. 

The city-states of Sanyan were careful to maintain their independence from the Owuro state. While they traded extensively with the empire, they avoided political entanglement and maintained their own military forces, including proto-fleets of war canoes, which carried [[W:Archers|archers]] and foot soldiers capable of defending the islands. The sea that allowed Sanyan to connect with its trading partners also served as it's main line of defense. The first major external threat to Sanyanian independence came in [[1087|1087 CE]], when [[Imfezzan people|Imfezzan]] raiders launched a major attack on the archipelago. The Imfezzans, a nomadic people that had migrated for the [[Sundanese desert]] in the [[9th century]], had been raiding settlements along the [[Central Kaftia|Central Kaftian]] coast for decades, disrupting trade and seizing plunder and people that were then [[Slavery in Kaftia|forced into slavery]]. Centralization of the [[Owuro Empire|Owuro State]] was in part as a response to escalating raids by the Imfezzans, with the Oba commanding tighter control over his tributaries and demanding higher [[W:Tribute|tribute]] and [[W:Levies|levies]].

The raid of 1087 was the largest Imfezzan attack on Sanyan to date. A substantial fleet of raiding vessels descended on the islands, catching the city-states by surprise. Several coastal settlements were sacked before the Sanyanians could organize an effective defense. The defense was ultimately coordinated by the Ọba-Òkun of Tòkunbọ́, who assembled a fleet from multiple city-states and successfully drove off the raiders in a series of naval engagements. The victory demonstrated both the vulnerability of the divided city-states and their potential strength when united.

The 1087 raid had lasting consequences, prompting increased fortification of coastal settlements, greater cooperation among the city-states in matters of defense, and contributed to a growing sense that the existing political fragmentation was dangerous in the face of external threats. Relations with Burawa were conducted primarily through Owuro intermediaries, though direct contacts increased over time. The Burawa rulers [[Marcel II of Burawa|Marcel II]] ([[1098]]–[[1132]]), [[Iacob III of Burawa|Iacob III]] ([[1132]]–[[1161]]), and [[Andrias II of Burawa|Andrias II]] ([[1161]]–[[1185]]+) were known to Sanyanian merchants and scholars, and Burawa goods appeared in Sanyanian markets.

=== Tiwalade and unification (1126–1137) ===
{{Main|Tiwalade of Sanyan|Unification of Sanyan}}

By the early 12th century, the pressures driving unification had intensified. The Owuro Empire was becoming increasingly powerful and centralized under rulers such as [[Adebayo of Owuro|Adebayo]], [[Tiyamiyu of Owuro|Tiyamiyu]], and [[Akiniku of Owuro|Akiniku]]. While Owuro had traditionally been content with a commercial relationship with Sanyan, there were signs that the empire had expansionist ambitions, and would likely seek to assert control over the wealthy archipelago. At the same time, innovations from [[Southwestern Kaftia|Southern Kaftia]] were spreading north through the [[Empire of Burawa]] under rulers like [[Marcel II of Burawa|Marcel II]] ([[1098]]–[[1132]]), reaching Owuro and potentially threatening Sanyan's commercial position. The city-states, with their fragmented governance and competing interests, were relatively ill-equipped to navigate the changing political landscape. Into this context emerged [[Tiwalade of Sanyan|Tiwalade]], a figure whose historicity is debated but whose impact was profound. 

Historical records, including contemporary chronicles and later compilations of oral tradition, agree on the basic outline of Tiwalade's career: As a noble of ''Tòkunbọ́'', Tiwalade rose to prominence in the 1120s, initially as a military leader who distinguished himself in actions against [[Imfezzan raiders]] and in the defense of trade routes with the Owuro. Tiwalade's reputation for military skill, combined with diplomatic acumen and personal charisma, allowed the accumulation of a substantial following amongst the archipelago. The legends that surround Tiwalade add color and detail. The tales of the [[arokin]] tell of Tiwalade's divine selection by Olókun, of miraculous victories against overwhelming odds, and of wise judgments that established patterns of governance still followed centuries later. While most of these stories cannot be verified, they reflect the significant impact that Tiwalade had on the development of a Sanyanian consciousness.

Tiwalade's first challenge was to secure control of Tòkunbọ́ itself. The city's existing Ọba-Òkun and ruling council were not necessarily hostile to unification, but they were protective of their traditional prerogatives. Tiwalade's approach was to work within existing institutions, using a combination of persuasion, alliance-building, and carefully targeted pressure to build support for a unified Sanyanian state.

By 1128, Tiwalade had been appointed as a military commander with authority to coordinate defense across multiple city-states—an unprecedented position that laid the groundwork for broader authority. With Tòkunbọ́ secured, Tiwalade turned to the western city-states. [[Ìlú-Ayo]], with its textile wealth and religious significance, was a crucial objective. The approach here was primarily diplomatic—Tiwalade offered the Ìlú-Ayo weavers protected access to markets and raw materials in exchange for acceptance of Tòkunbọ́'s leadership. In order to bring [[Ẹ̀yọ́-Ìbà]] into the fold, Tiwalade reportedly made a [[W:Pilgrimage|pilgrimage]] to the shrine of Olókun on [[Ìwọ̀rìkò Island]], seeking and reportedly receiving the blessing of the priesthood. This religious endorsement was crucial in legitimizing Tiwalade's broader claims across the archipelago. By the end of 1131, the western city-states had accepted Tiwalade's leadership, though not without resistance from factions that feared loss of local autonomy and wealth.

The northern city-states, including Ojore, proved significantly more resistant to unification. Ojore's scholarly traditions and connections to mainland intellectual centers (particularly those of the Dakari origin, then part of the [[Empire of Burawa|Burawi Empire]]) made its ruling elites skeptical of Tòkunbọ́'s commercial orientation. Several smaller northern settlements actively resisted, leading to military campaigns that were, according to chronicles, brief but decisive. The founding of the [[University of Ojore]] in 1129, guided by scholarly [[W:Islam|Muslim]] Imfezzans that had been captured and sold into slavery by Owuro would complicate the situation. Tiwalade recognized the university's potential value and offered guarantees of academic freedom and religious tolerance in exchange for Ojore's acceptance of unification. This approach proved successful, and Ojore became an important center of the unified state. This marked the final phase of unification, involving not military conquest but political engineering. Tiwalade would found new institutions that would cement the authority of a unified Sanyan while respecting local traditions and interests. 

The most important of these was the [[Ìgbẹ́-Alájèpà Òkun|''Ìgbẹ́-Alájèpà Òkun'']] ({{Literal translation|league of the sea traders}}), a confederation that brought together the merchant guilds of all the city-states under a common framework. The league standardized trade practices, negotiated common agreements with mainland partners, and created a mechanism for sharing the benefits of commercial prosperity. The position of ''[[Oloye]]'' ({{Literal translation|owner of the chieftaincy}}) emerged as a new form of central authority, distinct from the traditional [[Ọba-Òkun]] of the preceding individual city-states. The Oloye would serve as the supreme authority for the unified state, but would be selected by a council representing all major interests rather than inheriting the position.

On the basis of these institutional innovations, the formal unification of Sanyan was proclaimed in [[1137]]. Tiwalade was installed as the first Oloye, establishing a precedent for non-hereditary succession to supreme authority.

==== The trade league: Ìgbẹ́-Alájèpà Òkun ====
{{Main|Ìgbẹ́-Alájèpà Òkun}}

The trading league would be Tiwalade's most enduring institutional creation, bringing together the ''[[Alájèpà]]'' ({{Literal translation|those who mastered profit}})—the [[W:Merchant guild|merchant guilds]] that controlled Sanyan's trade in silk, cowrie shells, and other products—under a common framework. The league established standardized weights, measures, and commercial practices across the archipelago, and established a common system for negotiating with mainland trading partners, ensuring that Sanyan spoke with one voice in commercial matters. Perhaps most importantly, it created mechanisms for sharing commercial prosperity across the archipelago, reducing the economic competition that had previously divided the city-states.

The league was governed by a council of guild masters, with representation proportionate to each guild's economic contribution. This council advised the Oloye on commercial matters and had significant influence over state policy. The integration of commercial interests into the formal structure of government was a distinctive feature of Sanyanian political culture that would persist throughout the pre-colonial period.

=== Unified Sanyan (1137–1470) ===
The unification of 1137 did not immediately result in a fully integrated state. Differences in traditions, institutions, and identities persisted, and the authority of the Oloye had to be continuously negotiated and reinforced. The first decades after unification were marked by a slow process of institution-building and occasional (often violent) challenges to central authority. Tiwalade's successors as Oloye built upon the foundations established during the unification period. The [[Àjẹ́-Amọ̀ye]] council of kingmakers was formalized as the body responsible for selecting each new Oloye, with membership drawn from across the archipelago. The [[Ìwàrẹ́ fà]] judicial councils were expanded and standardized, creating a more uniform legal system.

The position of Ọba-Òkun continued at the local level, with each major settlement maintaining its own sacred chief responsible for local governance and religious functions. The relationship between the Oloye and the various Ọba-Òkun was defined through a combination of tradition and explicit agreement, creating a system that balanced central authority with local autonomy.

Post-unification Sanyanian society was highly structured and hierarchical, characterized by a sacred chief ([[Ọba-Òkun]]) at the top, balanced by councils of chiefs, lineage heads ([[Olórí-Ọ̀bọ̀]]), trade guilds, and secret societies. Power was not absolute; it was decentralized, with [[Ìjọyè]] overseeing islands and villages ([[obodo]]) while relying on the [[Àjẹ́-Amọ̀ye]] (kingmakers) or [[Ìwàrẹ́ fà]] (judicial/religious) councils to check their authority.

The social hierarchy included:

* '''[[Ọba-Òkun]]''' — Sacred chiefs who governed individual settlements and served as religious authorities
* '''[[Olórí-Ọ̀bọ̀]]''' — Lineage heads who represented major families and clans
* '''[[Alájèpà]]''' — Members of trade guilds who controlled commercial activities
* '''[[Àjẹ́-Àṣírí]]''' — Members of secret societies with roles in governance and social control
* '''[[Arokin]]''' — Griots who served as oral historians, advisors, and mediators
* '''Free citizens''' — Farmers, fishers, craftspeople, and traders
* '''Slaves''' — Owned by aristocracy and citizens, acquired through various means

[[Slavery in Sanyan|Slavery]] expanded significantly in the years following unification were owned by the aristocracy and by ordinary citizens. They were acquired in a number of ways: being purchased or traded from the Owuro (typically [[W:Prisoners of war|prisoners of war]] from the interior of Central Kaftia), through gifts to the Ọba-Òkun, who also inherited the slaves of those who died intestate, and by tribute paid by dependent territories to the Ọba-Òkun and prominent chiefs. Hardened criminals or those guilty of serious crimes were either executed or forced into slavery. The possession of a large number of slaves would become a symbol of an individual's high status in the Sanyanian hierarchy. Slaves additionally served in the militia and became the bulk of the labor force for the local chiefs. However, slavery in Sanyan was not a static condition—slaves could sometimes be [[W:Manumission|granted, earn or purchase their freedom]], and their children could be born free, although this depended on the family they were tied to.

===Illypnian contact===
{{Main|Ìlàlóye trade}}

By the time of the first recorded contact with Illypnian explorers, Sanyan was a relatively centralized state at the centre of an extensive trading network. Apart from natural resources and {{W|ivory}}, the country manufactured and traded copperware, {{W|ferrous}} metal goods, {{W|silk}} and {{W|raffia}} cloth, and {{W|pottery}}. The languages of Sanyan reflected the population of the {{W|merchants}}, largely {{W|Yoruba language|Imo}}, {{W|Ijaw language|Ijaw}}, {{W|Igbo language|Ekwe}} and to a less degree {{W|Ibibio language|Ibibio}}. 

With the arrival of Volisanian sailors to the shores of Sanyan in 1470, the people were at first very wary of the newly arrived foreigners. The culture shock was quite strong, especially because Illypnians were unfamiliar with the [[Kinza alphabet and writing system]] nor accustomed to the local traditions. In 1472, [[EXPLORER]] left some of his sailors in Sanyan and took several nobles to [[Volisania]]. He returned to Sanyan with the nobles in 1478; such commissioning, hiring, or even kidnapping of local Kaftians to use as local ambassadors, especially for newly contacted areas, was by then an already established practice. These exchanges of missions continued, peaking with the large [[Òrìṣáyọmí mission]] in 1665 which travelled extensively across the [[Crown of Volisania]] and [[Southwestern Kaftia]]. They remained for nearly four years, studying Christianity and learning reading and writing.

Leaders realized that the island was at an substantial disadvantage to the Illypnians, arriving in large warships with armaments and technology never before seen, and that far outclassed everything possessed by Sanyan. The leaders of Sanyan aimed to strengthen the country against the threat of potential colonization, resulting in the island "throwing open its doors to foreign technology." At its heart, this societal shift embodied the absorption of foreign ideals and technology (collectively referred to as ''àjèjì'', meaning "foreign") and fusing them with Sanyanian ideas in the name of self-preservation.
[[File:Antetokoumpo 16th century.png|left|thumb|The ''Ìlàlóye'' trade with Illypnians was strictly limited to the district at [[Tokunbo|Tòkunbọ]]]]
The Ìlàlóye trade trade was the most significant result of Illypnian contact began with the state now known to Mesogean explorers, {{W|missionaries}}, and {{W|merchants}} whom established long-distance overseas trade routes with the city. The resulting cultural exchange included the introduction of {{W|gunpowder}} and {{W|matchlock firearms}}, the {{W|printing press}}, and {{W|Christianity}} to Sanyan, and the island rapidly developed into a transit node for Illypnian slave ships. By the early seventeenth century the port at [[Tòkunbọ]] and its hinterland had a constant population of around 200 Illypnian traders, while the island of a whole had around 100,000. This concentration allowed resources, soldiers and surplus foodstuffs to be readily available at the request of the Oloye. This made the position extremely powerful and caused the state to become highly centralized around him.

This substantial rise in consciousness, latter referred to as the "[[Self-Strengthening Movement]]" ({{Langx|kzi|ߌߦߌߞߊ ߕߌߕߎ߲ ߊߜߓߊߙߊ ߊߙߊ|Ìyíká Títún Agbára Ara}}) was successful in that it brought focus to the threat posed to the sovereignty and traditions of Sanyan by the enriching Illypnian explorers and merchants. Not only through an explosion of knowledge, but on a sociological level, the urgency of this period redefined how Sanyan viewed both itself and its place in the shrinking world. The radical uplifting urged by Oloye initiated a shift in Sanyanian society, one from more close-knit {{W|tribalism|tribal groups}}, to one of urban development marked by cosmopolitan sophistication. This new identity led to a greater social consciousness, and Sanyan became a cultural and trading powerhouse in Kaftia, expanding intellectual and social contacts internationally.

Sanyan had long forcibly enslaved {{W|prisoners of war}} it captured and transferred them to the island as a way to increase the workforce and tribute-paying subjects. It is this earlier system that rapidly enlarged the city, which had become embedded in a densely settled immediate hinterland, and it was the same mechanism of enslavement and transfer of population that made Sanyan able to sell captives outside the country.

Although initially Sanyan prohibited the transport of slaves through the island, following the development of a successful Volisanian sugar-growing colony on the territory of [[Bonande]], the island became a major transit point for their traders. The {{W|Cantino planisphere}} of 1502 mentions Sanyan as a center for the transit and sale of slaves for the island of [[ba]] but notes that Sanyan provided few itself. Debt bondage and military slavery had existed in Sanyan long before the arrival of the Illypnians, and almost entirely consisted of captives from Sanyan's wars against [[Owuro Empire|the Owuro]]. 

During the [[Kaftian slave trade]] the traditional knowledge of the inshore waters of the western coast of Kaftia and nautical experience led to many Sanyanian employed as sailors, navigators and interpreters aboard slave ships, as well as to [[Bayani Baliscans|Bayani]], Volisanian, [[Maroon pirates|Maroon]] and eventually Baliscan warships used against the slave trade. To ensure their status as “freemen,” they initiated the practice of tattooing their foreheads and the bridge of their nose with indigo dye to distinguish them from slave labor. Many were  hired as free sailors on Illypnian ships, initially engaged in the slave trade, and then when that ended in the coastal trade in goods. They were famous for their skills in navigating and sailing the Ethiopic Ocean. Their maritime expertise evolved along the [[Bight of Ayorin]] where they made a living as fishermen and traders. 
====Opposition to slavery====
{{See also|Maroon Wars|Afro Atlantic Charter|Maroon pirates}}
[[File:Jaspar Beckx (tidl. tilskrevet) - Don Miguel de Castro, Emissary of Kongo - KMS7 - Statens Museum for Kunst.jpg|thumb|200px|[[Nsuukidem Jesumbo]], the Sanyanian ambassador to the [[Maroon Confederacy]] (1642)]]
[[File:Oath_of_the_Ancestors_painting.png|left|thumb|360x360px|[[Oath of the Ancestors|Juramento de los Ancestros]]  (1886) depicts a [[Maroon Wars|Maroon]] (left) and a [[W:Yoruba people|Imo]] woman (right) symbolizing the shared [[Afro Atlantic Charter|oath to resist against slavery]]]]
Oloye [[Ekpedeme]] died in 1652, and was replaced by [[Nsuukidem of Sanyan|Nsuukidem]]. Nsuukidem, the son of former nobles, had himself served as an ambassador to the [[Maroon Confederacy]] and witnessed the cruelty of the {{W|chattel slavery}} that Sanyan was facilitating. He strongly denounced the practice, and threatened to end the arrangement. He was convinced otherwise by his advisors, who reminded him of the significant power imbalance between the foreign powers and Sanyan. In order the overcome this weakness, disrupt the slave trade, and quickly allow the prosecution of intercepted slavers and their vessels, Nsuukidem, supported by his council, and through his ties to figures within the [[Araucarian Passage]], financed the [[Maroon pirates]] to halt intercept and liberate Illypnian slave ships on their way to the Algarves. This was already a common practice of the [[Maroon pirates]] dating back to the founding of [[Daramazán]] in 1617, however as shown by historical records, pirate attacks intensified in the years after 1643.

This mutual alliance between the two sides was solidified by the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]] of 1793 during the conflict.

The Ìlàlóye trade was significantly reduced with the [[Volisanian Civil War]] and the beginning of the [[Baliscan War of Independence]]. The trade completely evaporated in 1808 with [[Baliscan Kaftian Squadron]] launching the [[Kaftian anti-slave trade campaign]] from [[Joint Base Ajowa|Ajowa Naval Base]] in Sanyan. Western Kaftian slave markets, through which a majority of slaves sent to the Algarves had passed, largely collapsed following Volisania and Breisland halting the trade in 1815, and the outbreak of the [[Azawagh War]] was the {{W|coup de grâce}} for the slave trade. Oloye [[Kuyikabasi of Sanyan|Kuyikabasi]] officially abolished all forms of slavery that same year.
[[File:Arch of Return (Ògbómù Ìpadà).jpg|thumb|The [[Arch of Return (Sanyan)|Arch of Return]] (''Ògbómù Ìpadà''), which welcomed repatriated Kaftians from Abyala to Sanyan]]
Following the victory of the Baliscan revolutionaries, the Maroon Confederacy supported Sanyan's efforts to return newly freed Kaftians to their places of origin via the island. The arrangement was a substantial failure on several fronts: the displaced Kaftian who sailed to the island were often unable to ever return to their original homes on the continent. Additionally, upon arrival in Sanyan, each recaptive was given a registration number, and information on their physical qualities would be entered into the [[Register of Liberated Kaftians]]. Oftentimes the documentation would be overwhelmingly subjective and would result in inaccurate entries, making them difficult to track. Further investigation revealed additional disparities in the entries of the recaptives, specifically in the names; many recaptives felt pressured to adopt [[Kinza]] names to avoid potential maltreatment, which contributed greatly to the difficulty in locating them after they arrived. 

Most significantly, however, was fatality rate from {{W|tropical diseases}}. Mortality from [[W:Tropical diseases|tropical diseases]] was high—of the 12,823 who arrived in Sanyan between 1650 and 1800, only 6277 survived. However, by the conclusion of the [[Abyalan Wars of Independence]], over 350,000 captured Kaftians had been freed by the scheme.  

The [[Ani Federali|Baliscan government]] led by [[Michelangelo Valentino]] recognized the Kafto-Ethiopian Charter, resulting in it becoming public knowledge to the Illypnian powers for the first time. Balisca, although saddled with immense war debt and political gridlock, put an end to the repatriations upon learning of its failures in 1839. It upheld many of the other obligations to Sanyan that were also assured by Araucaria. 

=== Oil discovery and early industrialization ===
{{See also|Cosmocazo||}}

[[Baliscan Revolution|Social upheaval in Balisca]] heralded significant political shifts on both sides of the Ethiopic, with the government of Sanyan undertaking an immense program of nationalization and economic reforms. Most notable was the nationalization of the [[Kafto-Araucarian Petroleum Company]], which had begun oil exploration within Sanyan in 1908, and had since grown substantially. It was able to continue it's growth throughout the [[W:First World War|First World War]], due to Balisca's (and by extension, Sanyan's) neutrality.
[[File:Oil drilling in Sanyan (1908).png|thumb|268x268px|Discovery wells at Joloyema in south-western Sanyan, where oil was struck in September 1908 (1920)]]
During this time several cultural movements arose from a generation that had been educated in Balisca, and thus had access to [[Accelerationism|accelerationist]] philosophy and cultural works. A majority of them benefited from paternalist investment in cultural capital, including better-than-average education. Many in this new movement were students, writers, and artists returning from long-established higher education institutions in Balisca. Armed with their education, this new generation of Sanyanians sought to create better standard of living for their society. At its heart, this movement embodied the desire to bring Sanyan into [[W:Futurism|the emerging future]].

====Sanyan Emergency====
{{Main|Sanyan Emergency}}

The Sanyan Emergency denotes a historical period characterized often violent clashes by predominately [[Imo people|Imo]] and [[Ekwe people|Ekwe]] communities that ultimately led to the abolition of the existing social caste system and the expansion of citizenship laws. The Sanyanian caste system emerged in the 19th century as a result of the deliberate exclusion of [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter|repatriated Kaftians]] (from Abyala) from positions of power and influence. This exclusionary practice consolidated into a rigid social hierarchy that dictated opportunities based on an individuals' birth (tribal heritage).

The abolition of the caste system became a central objective of the uprising, with citizens advocating for a more egalitarian society. The culmination of these efforts led to a restructuring of Sanyan's social fabric, marked by the elimination of caste-based distinctions and the enactment of broader citizenship laws. This transformative period in Sanyan's history signifies a departure from historical inequities, fostering a more inclusive environment where citizenship is not constrained by arbitrary social categorizations.

One of the largest protests took place in 1946; it drew 2,000 participants, most of whom were Baliscan-educated intellectuals allied with [[Kaftian nationalism|Kaftian nationalists]] and dissatisfied oil workers. During another protest which took place in August 1946, participants chanted Pan-Kaftian and anti-imperialist slogans, calling for a social revolution and the abolition of tribal-based discrimination. In October, protesters successfully sabotaged oil pipelines across the island by destroying the pipelines with bulldozers.

===Decolonization of Kaftia===
{{Main|Decolonisation of Kaftia}}

As the [[Adonian Community]] embraced decolonization, Sanyan began a leader in the [[Decolonization of Kaftia|Kaftian liberation]] movement, often clandestinely providing aid to revolutionary groups across the continent. The newly formed [[Alaafia Party]] quickly rose to prominence, having campaigned heavily in favor of a rejection of foreign influence, in favor of promoting Sanyanian culture and values, while at the same time engaging with the increasingly globalized world. Economically, Alaafia included promises of nationalisation, economic planning, full employment, a [[National Health Service (Sanyan)|National Health Service]], and a system of social security. The election returned a landslide victory for Alaafia, and its leader, [[Kayin Oylekan]] assumed prime-ministership. At the same time, the 1960 Sanyan constitutional referendum was approved, restoring the directorial council with purely ceremonial roles, culminating in [[Oluwayemisi of Sanyan|Oluwayemisi]] being proclaimed as Oloye on 6 March 1960.
[[File:Noya 1960.png|left|thumb|284x284px|"Sanyanian shooter" a 1960 [[Noya]] illustration depicting a Sanyanian archer expelling a colonizer with an arrow ("Out of Kaftia!").]]
After taking office Oylekan and his cabinet had set forth a policy of neutrality in most global affairs, during lingering tensions between the powers involved in the [[Great Adonian War]]. Sanyan opted to remain neutral in explicitly supporting either [[W:Capitalism|capitalist]] or [[Socialist Bloc|socialist]] interests. Sayan continued to maintain its membership in the [[Kaftian Union]], possibly due to cold relations with [[Ayorin]], and later joined the [[Non-Aligned Movement]] in 1961 as a full member. Within the country, power remained largely in the hands of the Alaafia Party, while the smaller parties played only minor roles in governance. Oylekan's administration was both nationalist and socialist. Thus, it funded national industrial and energy projects, developed a strong national education system and promoted Sanyanian culture, while simultaneously cultivating {{W|Pan-Africanism|Pan-Kaftian sentiment}}.

Domestically, Oylekan led the nation through a period of rapid modernization, introducing reforms such as free university and healthcare for all residents of Sanyan, a relatively peaceful redistribution of land. The availability of medicine and access to clinics was vastly expanded, into the hopes of suppressing and ultimately eradicating {{W|tropical diseases}}. He oversaw the elevation of women to positions of power, while expanding the rights of immigrant workers and religious minorities (such as the [[Imfezzans in Sanyan|Imfezzani Muslims]]) alike, laying the groundwork for later Sanyanian cultural developments. The poverty rate amongst the population was halved within the first x years following the oil boom, and the government embarked on a program to expand access to housing and employment. An {{W|oil terminal}} and a {{W|deep water port}} were developed in [[Tokunbo|Tòkunbọ]], bridges, railways, hospitals and schools expanded throughout the country thanks to {{W|petrodollar recycling}}.

The progress—both symbolic and real—during this period became a point of reference from which Sanyan's community gained a sense of self-determination and unity that provided a growing sense of both urbanity and empowerment, as well as an example for the newly independent states to build upon on the push for {{W|decolonization}} in the 1950s and 1960s. The rapidly expanding urban setting of the island provided a venue for Sanyanians of all ethnic and linguistic backgrounds (most notably {{W|Yoruba people|Imo }}, {{W|Igbo people|Ekwe}} and [[Sanyan Creole people|Kíríyóòlù]] ["{{W|creole peoples|creole}}"]) to experience their diversity and establish solidarity with the wider Kaftian consciousness. Through this expression, the Renaissance encouraged the new appreciation of Sanyanian roots and culture. For instance, once-suppressed folk materials and teachings provided a rich source for the artistic and intellectual imagination, which provided a long sought connection to traditional Sanyanian culture. Through sharing in these cultural experiences, a consciousness took the shape of a Sanyanian identity and nation-state.

This huge rise in ethnic consciousness, latter referred to as the [[Kaftian Renaissance]] was successful in that it brought renewed focus to the status of [[W:African communalism|traditional communalist values]] that had been lost as a result of Illypnian colonialism and imperialism. Not only through an explosion of culture, but on a sociological level, the legacy of the Renaissance redefined how both Sanyanians and the rest of the world, viewed the fledgling state. The rapid industrialization of traditionally rural Sanyan sparked a cosmic shift in Sanyan's society, one from more close-knit tribal affiliations, to one of urban development marked by cosmopolitan sophistication. This new identity led to a greater social consciousness, and Sanyan would emerge as cultural powerhouse in Kaftia, expanding intellectual and social contacts across the [[Kaftian diaspora]].

