[[File:Ọna-Omi.png|left|thumb|300x300px|The skyline of the [[Olayema]] from [[Ọna-Omi]]. Sanyan has seen rapid expansion and {{W|verticalization}} since the oil boom began ({{Circa|2025}})]]
The economy of Sanyan is the ?th-largest in [[Southwestern Kaftia]], the ?th-largest in the [[Kaftia]], and it is the ?th-largest in the world. As of March 2023, Sanyan's gross domestic product (PPP) amounted to $63.671 billion, translating to a GDP per capita (PPP) of $67,827, the highest in Kaftia. Sanyan is a member [[COCO|Concepción Organization of Cooperation]] (COCO), an {{W|economic union}} committed to the establishment of an international cooperative system as an {{W|alter-globalization|alternative}} to the current system of {{W|corporate globalization}}, through the implementation of {{W|cooperative economics|cooperative economic ideals}}, such as {{W|mutualism}}, {{W|economic democracy}} and {{W|global justice movement|global justice}}.

Modern-day Sanyan is often described as an example of [[W:African communalism|Kaftian communalism]], reflecting its adherence to long-standing policy of [[Obodo àlàáfíà|''Obodo àlàáfíà'']] ("communal wellbeing"), which strives to establish an {{W|Socialist-oriented market economy|socialist-oriented market economy}} through {{W|African socialism|Kafto-socialist}} policies. This involves support for a universalist [[W:Welfare state|welfare state]] aimed specifically at enhancing individual autonomy and promoting {{W|social mobility}}, integrating representatives of labour in government, and labour market policy is mediated by the government and a commitment to Kaftian socialism tied to a large number of publicly-owned enterprises and social ownership in publicly listed firms. 

The state dominates in strategic "pillar" sectors such as energy production and {{W|shipping industry|shipping}}, but [[W:Cooperative economics|cooperative enterprise]] has expanded enormously, with around 3000 cooperative businesses recorded in 2022. In 2022, cooperative enterprises in Sanyan accounted for 40% of GDP, 70% of urban employment and 90% of new jobs.

In the decades after nationalization, the country was heavily reliant on oil revenue, and it was considered an early {{W|petrostate}}. The export of oil and gas as a share of GDP peaked in 1980 in the aftermath of the [[EVENT]] at over 90 per cent, and continues to account for 49.1 per cent of total economic output. About 923,478 barrels of oil are produced every day, making Sanyan the 23rd largest producer in the world, and the 3rd-largest producer of oil in [[Kaftia]]. It also produces approximately 18,936,844 million cubic metres (890×106 cu ft) of liquified natural gas per year, making Sanyan the 36th-largest gas exporter in the world. 

Substantial income from overseas investment supplements income from domestic production. Most of these investments are made by the [[Sanyan Investment Agency]], an arm of the [[Secretariat of Finance (Sanyan)|Finance Secretariat]]. 

Relative to its population, Sanyan features a large foreign labour market system that is conditional on stringent visa rules. Economic growth has been attributed in large part to the high number of foreign workers coming to Sanyan from [[Ayorin]], and the government has worked to reform the immigration process. With the impact of the {{W|COVID-19 pandemic}}, the decline of the {{W|employment-to-population ratio}} amongst citizens has accelerated. Prevailing Between 2010 and 2012, the government attempted to combat unemployment with supply-side reforms, but was met with fierce resistance; these reforms were never implemented. The low employment rate of nationals, particularly evident among {{W|young people}} have been blamed on {{W|minimum wages}} that would prevent "low productivity" workers from easily entering the labour market and overly-generous [[Social protection in Sanyan|government benefits and subsidies]]. Sanyan has begun testing a potential shift to {{W|automation}} of work, and has embraced the expansion of {{W|AI takeover|artificial intelligence}} in the workplace. 

Due to a lack of available land for farming, agriculture is not significant in the economy. Food is almost entirely imported to Sanyan. The state has been heavily investing in {{W|mariculture}} and the emerging {{W|offshore aquaculture}} practice to make up a portion this deficit.

===Energy, infrastructure, and environment===
{{Main|Energy in Sanyan|Transport in Sanyan|Environment of Sanyan}}

[[File:Sanyan wind turbines.png|left|thumb|An offshore [[W:Offshore wind power|wind farm]] in the [[W:Bight of Benin|Bight of Benin]]]]
Sanyan is the ?- largest producer of {{W|wind energy}} in Kaftia. Electricity generated in Sanyan is 40% from [[W:Nuclear power|nuclear power]], 33% from {{W|solar power}}, 19% from {{W|wind power}}, 8% from {{W|tidal power}}, and 2% from {{W|biomass}} resulting in a nearly CO2-free electricity-generating network. The government of Sanyan also enforces energy conservation, green technologies, emission reduction activities, and aims to meet the country's electricity demands using 100% renewable sources by 2026. Internationally, Sanyan is committed to the [[Okami Protocol]] and several other treaties promoting {{W|biodiversity}}, low emission standards, {{W|water management}}, universal {{W|recycling}}, and the {{W|renewable energy commercialization}}.

Infrastructure has rapidly improved since the beginning of the oil era, with the government spending billions from resource extractions on paving roads, and the construction of airports and the development of oil terminals and the [[Port of Sanyan]]. With these generous subsidies, Sanyan's infrastructure has rapidly become some of the most developed in the region. Sanyan additionally has one of the lowest rates of car ownership in Kaftia and the world, while also having one of the highest percentages of [[W:Plug-in electric vehicles|plug-in electric vehicles]] out of the total number of vehicles. Rising immigration, {{W|sea level rise}}, and rapid urbanization has further discouraged dependence on roads and increasing shifts to [[W:Mass transit|mass transit]] and active modes of travel.

=== Resources ===
[[File:Sanyan oil platform.jpg|thumb|276x276px|An [[W:Oil platform|oil platform]] (Sanyan-3) off the eastern coast of Sanyan]]
Beginning in early 20th century, joint Balisca-Sanyanian authorities discovered huge [[W:Crude oil|crude oil]] resources in the waters off the coast of the [[Amara Delta]]. Production rapidly increased amidst the {{W|1973 oil crisis}} and the subsequent economic shock. The export of oil and it's derived products has generated enormous revenues for the state for the last three decades, adding hundreds of billions of dollars to the government's budget. However, the unforeseen consequences of the country's huge energy wealth impacted the competitiveness of other sectors of the economy, leading to it developing a case of {{W|Dutch disease}}.

Export revenues from oil and gas have risen to over 30% of total exports and constitute almost 20% of the GDP. 1965, the Sanyanian government established the {{W|social wealth fund}} (the [[Owó-àpò Àlàáfíà]]), which would be funded with oil and gas revenues, including taxes, dividends, sales revenues and licensing fees. This was intended to reduce overheating in the economy from oil and gas revenues, minimize uncertainty from volatility in prices, and provide a cushion to compensate for expenses associated with the expanding social safety net.

The government controls its petroleum resources through a combination of state ownership in major operators in the oil and gas fields and the fully state-owned [[Kafauco|Sanyanian Oil Company]]. Importantly, the government controls licensing of exploration and production of fields. The fund invests in developing financial markets outside of Sanyan, mostly in Kaftia other regions in the [[Periphery countries|global periphery]]. Spending from the fund is constrained by budgetary rules, which limits spending over time to no more than the real value yield of the fund, originally assumed to be 4% a year, but raised in 2021 to 5% of the fund's total value.

The investment choices of Sanyanian social fund are directed by {{W|ethics code|ethical guidelines}}; for example, the fund is not allowed to invest in companies that produce parts for conventional or nuclear weapons, utilize forced or {{W|child labor}}, and any other forms of {{W|human rights abuse}}. Sanyan's highly transparent investment scheme has been lauded by international organizations, and efforts to replicate it across the world have been underway since at least 2010. The future size of the fund is closely linked to the price of oil and to developments in international financial markets.

== Geography and climate ==

=== Nature and islands ===
{{Main|Wildlife of Sanyan}}

[[File:Forest of São Tomé Island.jpg|thumb|Tropical vegetation in Sanyan's [[Ọgbà Òdòdó Nature Reserve]]]]
The wildlife of Sanyan is surprisingly diverse despite its rapid urbanization. The majority of fauna that remain on the island exist in [[W:Nature reserve|nature reserves]] such as the [[Ọgbà Òdòdó Nature Reserve]] and the [[Ẹ̀pàdà Omi Wetland Reserve]]. In 1818, just prior to the [[Continuation War|beginning]] of [[Afro Atlantic Charter|repatriations from the Algarbes]], Sanyan was mostly covered in [[W:Rainforest|rainforests]]. During that time, it still contained flora shared with the [[Amara Delta]] and other areas around the [[Bight of Ayorin]], but even then, the biodiversity of fauna was relatively low. Following the establishment of the Maroon colonies, rapid deforestation began due to crop cultivation, and was largely completed by the 20th century. By some estimates, there has been a loss of 75% of the natural habitats of Sanyan over the course of the past 200 years. Due to the deforestation, over 20 species of freshwater fish, 100 species of bird, and a number of mammals became locally extinct. A 2003 estimate put the proportion of extinct species as over 28%.

Sanyan's urbanisation means that it has lost 95% of its historical forests, and now over half of the naturally occurring fauna and flora in Sanyan is present in nature reserves, which comprise only around 8% of Sanyan's land area. In 1960, to combat this decline in natural space, the government introduced the vision of making Sanyan a "garden city", aiming to substantially improve quality of life. Since then, nearly 20% of Sanyan land has been set aside for parks and nature reserves, while green space (including urban) makes up 38% of Sanyan's territory. 

==== Named islets and barrier islands ====
Several smaller islands held historical significance in pre-colonial Sanyan:

* '''[[Ìwọ̀rìkò Island]]''' — A small islet off the eastern coast of Olayema, traditionally associated with the priesthood of [[Olokun]] and site of important religious ceremonies
* '''[[Ààrín-Òmì]]''' ({{lang|kz|"middle of the waters"}}) — A barrier island that served as a natural breakwater for the main harbor
* '''[[Ìdánwò]]''' ({{lang|kz|"test"}}) — A rocky islet used historically for ritual trials and coming-of-age ceremonies
* '''[[Ẹ̀rín-Ìlè]]''' — An islet in the western lagoon known for its freshwater springs

=== Climate ===
Sanyan experiences a [[w:Tropics|tropical]] {{W|climate}}, specifically a {{W|tropical monsoon climate}} (Am) under the {{W|Köppen climate classification}}. A [[W:Wet season|wet season]] with heavy monsoonal downpours runs from November to May, with a relatively [[W:Dry season|dry season]] from June to October, though light showers occur during this period.

The [[W:Harmattan|''harmattan'']], which climatically influences many cities in [[Central Kaftia]], is less pronounced in Sanyan. The country's heaviest precipitation occurs during September with an average of 367 millimetres or 14.45 inches of rain. December on average is the driest month of the year, with an average rainfall of 20 millimetres or 0.79 inches. Temperatures throughout the year in the city are relatively constant, showing little variation throughout the course of the year. Average temperatures are typically between 25 and 28 °C (77.0 and 82.4 °F) in the city.

Sanyan, like other island nations, recognizes that [[W:Climate change|climate change]] and [[W:Sea level rise|rising sea levels]] in the decades ahead will have major implications for its low-lying coastline. It estimates that the nation will need to spend $100 billion over the course of the next century to address the issue. In its 2020 budget, the government set aside an initial $8 billion towards a Coastline and Flood Protection Fund. Sanyan is the first country in Central Kaftia to levy a [[W:Carbon tax|carbon tax]] on its largest carbon-emitting corporations producing more than 25,000 tons of carbon dioxide per year, at $85 per ton (in 2020), which will rise to $120 per ton by 2030.{{Weather box
