=== Education ===
Sanyan's educational system is divided into preschool (for those under age 6), basic education (9 years, in three stages, compulsory), secondary education (3 years, compulsory since 1968), and higher education (subdivided in university and polytechnic education). 
[[File:University of Ojore.png|thumb|300x300px|The campus of the [[University of Kofi|University of Ojore]]. It is the largest university in Sanyan]]
The first university was established in 1129, as the [[University of Ojore]]. With the beginning of the oil era, access to education was rapidly expanded, as it was regarded as the foundation of a successful society. {{W|Free education}} was first open to every citizen of Sanyan in 1962, and was extended to all Kaftian expats in 2016. However, students who chose to attend private schools are still supported with public money. With the increasing tuition fees a student has to pay to attend a private run higher education institution and the attraction of new types of students (many as part-time students or in evening classes) like employees, businessmen, parents, and pensioners, many departments make a substantial profit from every additional student enrolled in courses, with benefits for the college or university's gross tuition revenue and without loss of educational quality (teacher per student, computer per student, classroom size per student, etc.).

Since then, the country has progressed with efforts of ensuring high literacy rates, modern programs and women’s share in education. Sanyan currently devotes approximately 25 percent of total {{W|government spending}} to education. Annual education investment went from less than $<> billion in 2000 to more than $<> billion in 2020, or #% of Sanyan's gross domestic product. However, there remains an inequality in education spending. 
[[File:Sanyan Donning of the siilu.jpg|thumb|A [[wikipedia:Kente cloth#Modern use of kente|donning of the academic ''siilu'' cloth]] ceremony during graduation from Momoko University in 1973|left]]
Public education is free for all citizens through the university level in Sanyan. Expatriates from across [[Kaftia]] can also attend school in Sanyan for free, up to (but not including) the university level. Education is compulsory through the twelfth grade. Primary school enrollment for males in 2015 stood at 99% and 99% of females. Secondary school enrollment figures are lower with 96% for males, and 98% for females. In 2015–16 approximately 0.2 percent of students in grades one through five and 0.6 percent of students in grades six through nine did not complete their education; this rate rose to 0.9 percent in grades 10–12. The overall literacy rate of Sanyan stood at 96.5% in 2020. Between the ages of 15-29, males reached 99% literacy and women 100%. In 1960, only 40% of the population could read, compared to 85% in 1990.

The educational system is divided into preschool (for those under age 6), basic education (9 years, in three stages, compulsory), secondary education (3 years, compulsory since 1968), and higher education (subdivided in university and polytechnic education). Universities are usually organized into faculties. Institutes and schools are also common designations for autonomous subdivisions of Sanyan higher education institutions. There is no uniform curriculum across the Sanyan, with the determination left to the individual states. 

About 51% of college-age citizens (20 years old) attend one of Sanyan's higher education institutions (compared with 37% in 2010 and 29% in 2000). There is a large (albeit shrinking) gender gap: 60% of Sanyanian women go on to attain higher education, compared with 48% of Sanyanian men. In addition to being a destination for international students, Sanyan has also risen among the top places of origin for international students. All higher education students, both domestic and international, totaled 9,937 in 2020. Students from elsewhere in Kaftia made up the vast majority of all international students, accounting for 88% of all international attendees in 2016. Sanyan's universities typically maintain strong educational partnerships with [[Kafto-Baliscans|Kafto-Baliscan]], tba and <Indigenous> institutions, and exchange programs and opportunities to study abroad are common. 

Higher education in state-run educational establishments is provided on a competitive basis, a system of [[W:Numerus clausus|numerus clausus]] is enforced through a national database on student admissions. However, every higher education institution offers also a number of additional vacant places through other extraordinary admission processes for sportsmen, mature applicants (over 23 years old), international students, foreign students from Kaftia (especially from [[Sudanese Kaftia]]), degree owners from other institutions, students from other institutions (academic transfer), former students (readmission), and course change, which are subject to specific standards and regulations set by each institution or course department.

=== Religion ===

== Culture == 
{{Main|Culture of Sanyan}}
 
Despite its small size, Sanyan has a diversity of languages, religions, and cultures, and is often times called a microcosm of the cultural diversity of the Kaftian continent. Historically, the culture of Sanyan has been predominantly characterized by a distinctive synthesis of traditional [[W:African communalism|West Kaftian customs]] (mainly [[W:Yoruba people|Imo]], [[W:Igbo people|Ekwe]], [[Dakari people|Dakari]] origins), with smaller currents of [[Sudanese people|Sudanese]] and [[Kaftian diaspora|Kaftian diasporic]] influence. Often described by sociologists as "[[W:Afrofuturism|Kafto]] or [[W:Africanfuturism|Kaftian-futurist]]", contemporary Sanyanian society has embraced the integration of high technology with indigenous ecological practices and communalist values. The concept of ''Obodo àlàáfíà'' ({{Literal translation|communal wellbeing}}) underpins this national ethos, prioritizing collective access to resources, [[W:Right to the city|public spaces]], and cultural heritage over private exclusivity. This philosophy is visibly manifest in the daily life of the populace, from the organization of residential wards to the state's management of the arts.

At the dawn of the [[Sanyan Emergency]] in 19xx, most residents of Sanyan were transient [[W:Migrant laborers|migrant laborers]] who had no intention of staying permanently and were forced into a discriminatory [[W:Caste system|caste system]]. This hierarchy restricted access to citizenship, social mobility and other rights based on the tribal or ethnic origins of an individual. As a result, and with the exception of the [[Sanyan Creole people|Sanyanian Creoles]] who had developed a longstanding attachment to the archipelago, most of the laborers' loyalties lay with their respective homelands of [[Ayorin]], [[Tamazgha]], [[Enfolo]] and other countries in Central and Northern Kaftia. After the Emergency, the government began a deliberate process of crafting a uniquely Sanyanian identity and culture, guided by Kaftian socialist ideals and building upon the polyethnic character that it maintained since the 18th century.

The national flower of Sanyan is the common water hyacinth, ''[[W:Pontederia crassipes|Pontederia crassipes]]'', which was likely introduced to the territory by repatriated Kaftians from Abyala in the 1800s. Sanyan is popularly known as the ''Panther City'' and ''Cowrie Island'' and many national symbols such as the coat of arms and the national flag make use of [[W:Cowrie|cowrie shells]]. 

