=== Sport ===
{{See also|Sanyan national football team}}
[[File:Sanyan National Stadium.png|thumb|Sanyan's 55,000-seat [[National Stadium (Sanyan)|National Stadium]] in the [[Olayema|Olayema district]]]]
[[W:Association football|Association football]] is the most popular sport in Sanyan, serving as a major unifying force for it's diverse population. The national team, known as the ''Ẹ̀fúùfù-nlá'' ({{Literal translation|divine wind}}), plays its home matches at the [[National Stadium (Sanyan)|National Stadium]] in Olayema. This 55,000-seat facility is architecturally significant, having been constructed entirely from [[W:Mass timber|mass timber]] and recycled steel salvaged from decommissioned oil platforms and completed in 2024.

Beyond football, traditional sports maintain a strong following. ''[[W:Dambe|Dambe]]'', a traditional [[W:Hausa people|Sakkwatáwa]] form of boxing, and [[W:Capoeira|capoeira]] (an Ayori-Baliscan-Asikyiran export) have been widely adopted into a regulated professional sport, with matches held in dedicated sand-filled arenas scattered across the city. 

Additionally, Sanyan's insular geography has fostered a strong culture of aquatic sports, and indoor and water sports are some of the most popular sports in Sanyan. Sanyanian sailors have had success on the international stage, with their [[Ọmọ-Yànsàn]] team being considered among the best in the world. Despite its size, the country has dominated swim meets in the [[Pan Kaftian Games]], alongside the traditional powerhouses [[Burawa]] and [[Orma]].

=== Public holidays and festivals ===
{{Main|Public holidays in Sanyan}}

The Sanyanian calendar incorporates a mixture of secular state holidays, traditional [[W:Yoruba religion|Òrìṣà]] observances, and [[W:Pan-Africanism|Pan-Kaftian celebrations]]. Republic Day, observed on 6 March, marks the restoration of the Oloye and the ratification of the 1960 constitution, typically celebrated with political speeches and a military parade. Freedom Day on 25 June commemorates the signing of the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]]; it is a solemn observance marked by the "Return to the Water" ceremony, where citizens gather at the beaches to pour libations in memory of ancestors who crossed the Ethiopic and those who did not survive the journey, and honoring those who jumped from [[W:Slave ship|slave ships]] in an act of resistance, where captives chose death over enslavement.

The most vibrant cultural festival is the [[Ogun Festival]] held in August. Dedicated to the Òrìṣà-Ifá [[W:Ogun|deity of iron]], it has evolved into a week-long celebration of technology, industry, and innovation. During this period, [[Trade unions in Sanyan|trade unions]] and tech collectives display their tools and innovations.

